.TH HELPBUOY-SERVER 1 "" "helpbuoy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
helpbuoy-server \- server for helpbuoy self-hosted remote support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B helpbuoy-server serve
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-state
.IR dir ]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie add
.I name
.I key
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie remove
.I name
.br
.B helpbuoy-server techie list
.br
.B helpbuoy-server computer list
.br
.B helpbuoy-server computer remove
.I fingerprint
.br
.B helpbuoy-server info
.RB [ \-\-company
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-phone
.IR number ]
.RB [ \-\-address
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-description
.IR text ]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server hostkey
.RB [ \-\-new ]
.br
.B helpbuoy-server version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B helpbuoy-server
is the SSH server of helpbuoy. Techies and the people they help connect to
it on one port; it authenticates their Ed25519 keys and splices their
sessions. The screen and input run end to end over an inner SSH connection
that the server cannot read.
.PP
.B serve
runs the server, by default on
.BR :5959 .
The other commands change or show the state directory; a running server picks
up changes within a second.
.PP
.B info
prints or sets who runs the server: the company name, phone number, address
and description that users waiting for a techie see. The company name and
phone number are required; until they are set the server refuses computers.
When they are missing and
.B serve
runs in a terminal, it asks for them first.
.B hostkey \-\-new
makes a new host key, used from the next server start on; every registered
computer then warns that the server identity changed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " addr"
Address to listen on, as host:port or :port.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " dir"
State directory with the host key, techies and registered computers. Default:
.B $HELPBUOY_SERVER_STATE
if set, else the per-user data directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HELPBUOY_SERVER_STATE
Default state directory. The Debian service sets it to
.IR /var/lib/helpbuoy-server .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR helpbuoy-serverctl (8)
